---
import Layout from "../components/Layout.astro";
import PageHeading from "../components/PageHeading.astro";
import BackLink from "../components/BackLink.astro";
import RetroKeyboard from "../components/RetroKeyboard.astro";
---

<Layout
    title="About - Andrew Hall"
    description="About Andrew Hall - Technologist and entrepreneur"
>
    <main class="max-w-6xl font-sans mx-auto px-8 py-12">
        <div class="max-w-prose mx-auto">
            <!-- Navigation -->
            <nav class="mb-12">
                <BackLink href="/" text="Home" />
            </nav>

            <!-- Header -->
            <header class="mb-12">
                <PageHeading text="About" />
            </header>
        </div>

        <!-- Content Section -->
        <section class="space-y-8">
            <div class="max-w-prose mx-auto">
                <p class="text-lg text-amber-950/90 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                    There is often a perceived divide between the creative and
                    the technical. I grew up in an environment where engineering
                    rigour and artistic expression were constantly overlapping,
                    so I never saw that divide. I learned early on that
                    structure and creativity are not opposing forces; they are
                    partners.
                </p>
                <p class="text-lg text-amber-950/90 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                    This duality defines my work. Whether it's an interactive 3D
                    experience, a mobile app or a complex financial platform,
                    the philosophy remains the same: the best technical
                    solutions aren't just functionally correct; they have to
                    feel right to the people using them.
                </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Keyboard -->
            <div class="flex justify-center mt-12 lg:mt-16 mb-8">
                <RetroKeyboard />
            </div>

            <div class="max-w-prose mx-auto">
                <h2 class="font-mono text-2xl text-amber-950 font-medium mt-16 mb-6">
                    From economics to engineering
                </h2>
                <p class="text-lg text-amber-950/90 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                    Software is an investment. Coming from a background in
                    economics rather than pure computer science, I view
                    development through a distinct lens: one of ROI, opportunity
                    cost, and resource allocation.
                </p>
                <p class="text-lg text-amber-950/90 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                    In an industry obsessed with the "new," it is easy to
                    over-engineer. The economic mindset shifts the question from
                    "Can we build this?" to "Should we?" It brings a discipline
                    to the process, ensuring that technical complexity is always
                    justified by clear business value.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="max-w-prose mx-auto">
                <h2 class="font-mono text-2xl text-amber-950 font-medium mt-16 mb-6">
                    Evidence over intuition
                </h2>
                <p class="text-lg text-amber-950/90 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                    This focus on value drives a rejection of guesswork. True
                    technical leadership isn't just about executing a roadmap;
                    it is about interrogating the problem.
                </p>
                <p class="text-lg text-amber-950/90 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                    Building the right thing requires closing the feedback loop
                    between product vision and technical reality. But evidence
                    isn't limited to API logs and retention metrics; it includes
                    the voice of the market itself.
                </p>
                <p class="text-lg text-amber-950/90 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                    Great products emerge when engineers are connected directly
                    with customers. By bringing market research and user
                    feedback right into the design and engineering space, we
                    drive the entire team to think commercially. This ensures we
                    aren't just solving abstract logic puzzles, but solving
                    validated business problems.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="max-w-prose mx-auto">
                <h2 class="font-mono text-2xl text-amber-950 font-medium mt-16 mb-6">
                    Velocity versus direction
                </h2>
                <p class="text-lg text-amber-950/90 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                    This rigourous approach is vital in the current AI
                    revolution. The democratisation of development is exciting
                    because it allows ideas to be tested faster than ever
                    before. But speed is not the same as direction.
                </p>
                <p class="text-lg text-amber-950/90 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                    When code is cheap, judgment becomes expensive. It is
                    critical to distinguish between a distracting experiment and
                    a viable feature. AI is a powerful accelerator, but it
                    requires a steady hand on the wheel to ensure rapid
                    prototyping creates a coherent path forward, rather than a
                    pile of disconnected tech debt.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="max-w-prose mx-auto">
                <h2 class="font-mono text-2xl text-amber-950 font-medium mt-16 mb-6">
                    Strategy in motion
                </h2>
                <p class="text-lg text-amber-950/90 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                    Ultimately, strategy cannot survive in a silo. It has to
                    live in the daily operations of the team.
                </p>
                <p class="text-lg text-amber-950/90 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                    Bridging the gap between the boardroom and the IDE is
                    essential. Business goals must translate into clear
                    technical requirements, and technical constraints must
                    inform business strategy. It isn't enough to just hand down
                    a vision; we have to build the team, the culture, and the
                    operational systems required to deliver it.
                </p>
                <p class="text-lg text-amber-950/90 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                    I am here to ensure that the vision isn't just a slide deck,
                    but a scalable, working reality.
                </p>
            </div>
        </section>
    </main>
</Layout>
